# IAX-9 — our own §6.10 re-anchoring, at both 0x8000 boundaries of a live call.
#
# Provenance: the outbound half of `wrap.pcap`, the same 77-second session as
# `live-wrap-inbound.hex`; see the provenance note there.
#
# Two windows, one per boundary, in capture order:
#
#  * **32768 ms.** Mini time-stamps run 32661 … 32761, then a **full Voice
#    frame at 32781**, then minis again from 32801. Nothing wrapped here — the
#    low 16 bits were nowhere near overflow — so this frame exists only because
#    §6.10 requires a full frame every 32,768 ms. It is the MUST being obeyed
#    where it costs something and buys nothing visible.
#
#  * **65536 ms.** Minis run 65401 … 65521, then a **full Voice frame at
#    65541**, then minis from 25. This is the same rule firing at the boundary
#    where it matters, and it is the §8.1.2 SHOULD as well.
#
# Both frames are ACKed by the node, so this also shows a full Voice frame
# behaving as a reliable frame mid-stream: it consumes an OSeqno (8 at the
# first boundary, 14 at the second) where the mini frames around it consume
# none.
#
# The replay test drives `IAX2VoiceTransmitter` with the 32-bit time-stamps
# read out of these datagrams and asserts it chooses full or mini exactly where
# the live client did — a regression pin on the one decision that, if it ever
# stopped firing, would leave a peer's expansion reference to drift forever
# with nothing on the wire to say so.
#
# Machine-generated. The captures themselves are not in the repository —
# they are the maintainer's own, and large. Regenerate with:
#   scripts/pcap-to-fixture.py wrap.pcap --stream 0 --dir out --range 3225:3245,6520:6545
# Capture SHA-256: 2658320d536ff332990c0d1d33571e0f7504366089476bddde8bec11772a3053
#
# Each datagram is the UDP payload verbatim — the octets RFC 5456 §5
# defines as an IAX2 frame. `[n]` is the datagram's index in the capture
# stream, so a gap in the indices is a datagram deliberately left out.

